--- body ---
## Context

`make check` is `test validate`, and `test` is `unit-test $(TEST_TARGETS)
check-store` over 40 suites. Make stops at the first failing target, so a run
that dies early reports the suites it reached and says nothing at all about the
rest. The output ends on the failing suite's own summary line, which reads like
the end of the run.

Measured on this host, 2026-09-16, before any of the raid's fixes:

```
contract: 16 passed, 1 failed, 0 warned, 1 skipped
make: *** [Makefile:56 : test-contract] Erreur 1
```

`contract` is the 29th entry in `TEST_SUITES`. The eleven after it --
`roundtrip`, `verify`, `stderr`, `install-staleness`, `encoding`,
`unknown_flags`, `selfcoherence`, `gofmt`, `determinism`, `strictwrite`,
`scopeconfinement` -- never executed, and nothing in the output says so.

## Why it clears the severity floor

Not because a partial run is slow to notice, but because it produced a wrong
diagnosis that survived most of a day.

With `test-contract` failing, `verify` was unreachable. When a host-level
cleanup cleared the stray `.git` and the whole run went green, `verify`'s
earlier FAIL was recorded in 0287's log as having "shared this cause". It had
not been retested; it had been *reached* for the first time. The raid's
Blind-Spot pass ran `make verify` standalone under live stray-`.git`
conditions and it passed, because its own `mktemp` workdir receives a `git
clone` that plants a valid `.git` inside it and stops Go's upward walk. The
two failures had different causes, and the cascade is what made them look like
one.

This is the repo's own recurring trap, in `make` form: an all-clear that cannot
be told from "I could not look". A contributor who reports "make check passed"
after an early failure has run a strict subset without being told which.

## Relevant files

- `Makefile:75` — `test: unit-test $(TEST_TARGETS) check-store`
- `Makefile:82` — `check: test validate`
- `Makefile:19` — `TEST_SUITES`, the ordered list

## Actions

Two options, not exclusive; the second is the cheap one and may be enough.

1. Run every suite and report at the end (`make -k`-style, or a driver loop
   that records each suite's exit code and fails at the end). Gives a complete
   picture per run, at the cost of running suites whose preconditions a failed
   earlier suite may have broken.
2. Keep fail-fast, but name the unreached suites on exit. A trap on the failing
   target prints the tail of `TEST_SUITES` that never started. Cheap, no change
   to run semantics, and it closes the specific gap that produced the
   misdiagnosis.

Pick one deliberately; do not do both by accident.

## Test

Red step: a fixture that forces an early suite to fail, asserting the run's
output names the suites that did not execute. Against today's Makefile it
fails -- the output ends at the failing suite.

Positive control: on a fully green run, the same assertion must find no
"unreached" list, or the check is reporting unreached suites that ran.

## Invariants

- `make check`'s exit code stays non-zero when any suite fails. This ticket
  changes what the run *says*, not what it *concludes*.
- No suite may be skipped to make the report tidy.

## Exit criteria

A `make check` run that stops early tells the reader which suites it did not
run. "It passed" and "it did not get there" stop looking alike.

## Related

- 0287 — the buildvcs defect whose diagnosis this gap corrupted. Fixed
  separately; this ticket is its Action 2, deliberately not folded in.
